## A Burial at Ornans: why was a funeral a scandal?
sources:
    - id: orsay
      title: Un enterrement à Ornans (RF 325)
      authors: Musée d'Orsay, Paris
      year: 2024
      finding: Between 1849 and 1850, oil on canvas, 315 × 668 cm; shown at the Salon of 1850–51; the villagers painted life-size, a size usually kept for history painting
    - id: wikifr
      title: Un enterrement à Ornans
      authors: Wikipédia (French)
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Painted in the family attic in Ornans, the canvas rolled up as he worked; sitters posed one by one (gravedigger Cassard, mayor Prosper Teste, a judge, a lawyer, his mother and sisters); letter to Champfleury of 50 life-size figures; Du Pays in L''Illustration: ''un amour du laid endimanché''; rejected from the 1855 Exposition universelle'
      url: https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Un_enterrement_%C3%A0_Ornans
    - id: wikien
      title: A Burial at Ornans
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Courbet's 'two old veterans of the Revolution of '93'; critics on its size, ugliness and rural subject; read by many as democratic after 1848
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Burial_at_Ornans
    - id: napoleon
      title: 'Polémiques et interprétations : une étude d''Un enterrement à Ornans'
      authors: Fondation Napoléon
      year: 2024
      finding: Original Salon title 'Tableau de figures humaines, historique d'un enterrement à Ornans'; the format of history painting for a genre scene
      url: https://www.napoleon.org/enseignants/documents/un-enterrement-a-ornans-1849-1850-de-gustave-courbet/

    card title "A Burial at Ornans"

Why would a funeral in a small country town cause a scandal in Paris?
    image art cites ["orsay","wikifr"] credit "Courbet, 1849–50. Musée d'Orsay, Paris. Public domain." x 500 y 395 width 980 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Gustave_Courbet_-_A_Burial_at_Ornans_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Gustave_Courbet_-_A_Burial_at_Ornans_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg look screen regions.beadles [0.335,0.29,0.08,0.42] regions.choir [0.205,0.47,0.065,0.53] regions.cliffs [0.02,0.15,0.96,0.12] regions.coffin [0.13,0.4,0.09,0.53] regions.cross [0.232,0.02,0.045,0.24] regions.crowd [0,0.2,1,0.8] regions.digger_grave [0.39,0.46,0.24,0.54] regions.dog [0.695,0.75,0.095,0.25] regions.far_left [0,0,0.3,1] regions.far_right [0.7,0,0.3,1] regions.grave [0.43,0.87,0.2,0.13] regions.gravedigger [0.39,0.46,0.11,0.42] regions.ground [0.3,0.62,0.45,0.38] regions.left [0.05,0.3,0.4,0.68] regions.men [0.44,0.19,0.16,0.72] regions.men_faces [0.44,0.25,0.16,0.14] regions.middle [0.3,0.3,0.4,0.68] regions.priest [0.265,0.375,0.1,0.6] regions.priest_book [0.325,0.44,0.05,0.07] regions.right [0.6,0.3,0.4,0.68] regions.skull [0.572,0.925,0.032,0.06] regions.vet_faces [0.59,0.33,0.12,0.17] regions.vet_view [0.45,0.3,0.4,0.68] regions.veterans [0.6,0.34,0.115,0.62] regions.women [0.68,0.2,0.32,0.78] regions.women_caps [0.7,0.23,0.25,0.11]

This is the painting that did it.
    highlight art:crowd strength soft

It shows his own home town: Gustave Courbet painted it in 1849 and 1850
    portrait courbet of "Gustave Courbet" cameo true

    erase courbet

in Ornans, in eastern France, where he was born.
    highlight art:cliffs strength soft

Start with its size: more than three metres tall, and almost seven metres wide.
    icon visitor size 250 set twemoji x 860 y 297 or ["person","standing"] icon_look picture of "person standing"
    text w_size "3.15 m × 6.68 m" #b5482e x 500 y 645 font_size 30

The mourners are life-siz (@highlight art:women_caps lift true@)e, a whole crowd of them; Courbet wrote of fifty figures.

    erase w_size

    quiz "In 1850, who got a canvas this size?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 560 buttons_at.y 560
        intro: So who usually got a canvas this big?
        right: Saints, kings and heroes: the top of the old ladder of subjects, history painting.
        wrong: Think of the top of the old ladder of subjects.
        + Saints, kings, heroes
        * Rich merchants
        * Village people

    erase visitor

Courbet gave that stage to his neighbours, and had them pose for him one by one.
    highlight art:men_faces lift true

    camera on ["art:left"] zoom 1.4

On the left stands the church: the priest reading the service,
    highlight art:priest_book lift true

under a cross held high.
    highlight art:cross lift true

Beside him stand two church officers in red, called beadles.
    highlight art:beadles lift true

    camera on ["art:middle"] zoom 1.4

Kneeling by the grave is the gravedigger, a poor local man, his jacket thrown on the earth.
    highlight art:gravedigger lift true

Behind him stand the town's men in black: the mayor, a judge, a lawyer friend of the painter.
    highlight art:men_faces lift true

    camera on ["art:right"] zoom 1.4

Two old men wear the breeches and stockings of the 1790s; Courbet called them veterans of the Revolution.
    highlight art:vet_faces lift true

And on the right, the women: Courbet's own mother and sisters lead them.
    highlight art:women_caps lift true

    camera reset true

    ask "Where is the grave? Tap it." spot grave on art
        intro: With all these people, the reason they came is easy to miss.
        right: There: a hole in the bare earth, with a skull on its rim.
        wrong: Look down, at the bare earth in the middle.

    camera on ["art:middle"] zoom 1.4

    focus art:skull strength strong rest blur duration 2.5

    camera on ["art:left"] zoom 1.4

The coffin hasn't even reached the grave; it's still being carried in on the left, under a white cloth.
    highlight art:coffin lift true

    camera on ["art:right"] zoom 1.4

No one is arranged around a hero.
    highlight art:women_caps lift true

Some weep, some stare ahead, and a  (@highlight art:dog lift true@)dog looks the other way.

    camera reset true

Shown at the Paris Salon at the end of 1850, it met a storm: critics called the people ugly and the scene trivial.
    highlight art:men_faces lift true

    camera on ["art:vet_view"] zoom 1.4

It came two years after the revolution of 1848, which gave every adult man in Fra (@highlight art:vet_faces lift true@)nce the vote, and many read it as a political picture.

    camera reset true

Courbet even claimed the top of the ladder: his Salon title called it the history of a burial at Ornans.
    highlight art:cross lift true

    pause 0.6

    draw visitor

That was the real offence: not ugly faces, but a country town given the size of history.
    focus art:crowd strength strong rest grey

    pause 1

    erase visitor

Bruegel's ploughman pushed a myth into the corner; here there is no myth at all, and a town's own funeral fills the wall.
    highlight art:gravedigger lift true

    quiz "A film-maker wants to do what Courbet did. Which plan follows his method?"
        right: The village council as a three-hour epic: ordinary people, given the scale kept for great events.
        wrong: Courbet changed two things at once: who is shown, and how big.
        * A short, quiet film about one farm
        + A three-hour epic of a village council
        * A ten-hour series on Napoleon
        * A grand film of a state funeral

Ordinary life no longer had to stand in a corner; it could fill the biggest wall in the Salon.
    highlight art:crowd strength soft lift true

